%!testlib
% The collector must trace a shared array's whole extent from a save
% record, not the length of the object the record was made for, run
% through `xpost -d null` by run-ps-test.sh: prints SUCCESS iff it holds.
%
% A getinterval view is a shorter object over a longer array entity.
% Writing through the view under a save copies the entity (copy-on-write)
% and records the write, and the save record carries the view's length.
% The collector marks the shared entity from that record; if it descended
% only the view's length it would leave the entity's tail untraced, and a
% child reachable only through the tail would be swept while the array is
% still live and still rooted -- a collector freeing a reachable object.
%
% Build an eight-element array of distinct children, a one-element view of
% it, drop every other root, write through the view under a save, force a
% collection, and require every child -- including the seven the view does
% not cover -- to still be readable. The verdict is left on the operand
% stack so it survives the restore, and judged after restoring.

/parent 8 array def
0 1 7 { parent exch 2 array put } for    % parent[i] = a distinct two-element child
/view parent 0 1 getinterval def         % a one-element view of the eight
0 1 300 { pop 1 array pop } for           % unrelated garbage for the collector to want

save
    view 0 42 put            % copy-on-write; the record is made for the view (length 1)
    1 vmreclaim              % collect while parent is still live and rooted
    % reading a child whose storage was swept raises rangecheck; a run
    % that reads every tail child without error is one where none was
    { 1 1 7 { parent exch get 0 get pop } for } stopped not
exch restore
    (a shared array's tail children survive a collection after a view write under save) assert

verdict
